content.text:
I would separate three concerns:

1. **Service identity** — the production Dapr app ID.
2. **Component usage** — which components call it.
3. **Development resolution** — how that service is mocked locally.

## 1. Declare platform services

```csharp
using Intropy.Topology;

public static class Services
{
    public static readonly ServiceRef Idempotency =
        ServiceRef.Define("idempotency-service");

    public static readonly ServiceRef BusinessIncidents =
        ServiceRef.Define("business-incident-service");
}
```

`ServiceRef` should contain only the stable Dapr identity. No image, port, Microcks configuration, or production deployment details.

## 2. Declare component usage

Every component builder would expose `.Uses(ServiceRef)`:

```csharp
public sealed class OrderFlowSystem :
    ISystemDefinition,
    IDevelopmentDefinition
{
    public string SystemName => "order-flow";

    public void Define(SystemBuilder builder)
    {
        builder.AddExtractor("order-extractor")
            .From(Connectors.Source)
            .Publishes(Topics.Orders)
            .Uses(Services.Idempotency)
            .Uses(Services.BusinessIncidents);

        builder.AddLoader("order-loader")
            .Subscribes(Topics.Orders)
            .To(Connectors.Destination)
            .Uses(Services.Idempotency);
    }

    public void DefineDevelopment(DevelopmentBuilder development)
    {
        // Local resolutions shown below.
    }
}
```

This gives the topology explicit edges:

```text
order-extractor → idempotency-service
order-extractor → business-incident-service
order-loader    → idempotency-service
```

Those edges become the `scopes` of generated Dapr resources.

## 3. Configure development mocks separately

My preferred DSL would be:

```csharp
public void DefineDevelopment(DevelopmentBuilder development)
{
    development.Mock(Services.Idempotency)
        .FromOpenApi(
            "mocks/idempotency-service.openapi.yaml",
            serviceName: "Intropy.IdempotencyService.API | v1",
            serviceVersion: "1.0.0");

    development.Mock(Services.BusinessIncidents)
        .FromOpenApi(
            "mocks/business-incident-service.openapi.yaml",
            serviceName: "Intropy.BusinessIncidentService | v1",
            serviceVersion: "1.0.0");
}
```

Paths would be relative to the SystemHost directory.

The DSL says “fulfil this dependency from an OpenAPI mock.” It does not expose Microcks directly. Microcks remains an Aspire backend implementation detail, like Redis does today.

## Generated development resources

The idempotency declaration would generate approximately:

```yaml
apiVersion: dapr.io/v1alpha1
kind: HTTPEndpoint
metadata:
  name: idempotency-service
spec:
  baseUrl: http://localhost:8585/rest/Intropy.IdempotencyService.API+%7C+v1/1.0.0
scopes:
  - order-extractor
  - order-loader
```

Business incidents would be scoped only to `order-extractor`.

The production profile generates neither endpoint. Dapr then performs normal discovery of the real app IDs.

## Materialized model

Conceptually:

```csharp
public sealed record ServiceResource
{
    public required string AppId { get; init; }

    public required IReadOnlyList<string> Consumers { get; init; }
}
```

Development configuration remains outside `SystemTopology`:

```csharp
public sealed record ServiceMock
{
    public required string AppId { get; init; }
    public required string ArtifactPath { get; init; }
    public required string ServiceName { get; init; }
    public required string ServiceVersion { get; init; }
}
```

This preserves an important boundary:

- `SystemTopology` describes production architecture.
- `DevelopmentManifest` describes local substitutions.

## Validation

I would add these rules:

- Service app IDs must be DNS-1123 labels.
- A component cannot declare the same service twice.
- A development mock must reference a service used by at least one component.
- A service may have at most one development resolution.
- Mock artifact paths must exist before the Aspire graph starts.
- Mock service name and version must be present.
- Service app IDs must not collide with component app IDs.
- A development run should fail early if a used service has no local resolution, rather than accidentally searching for a real service.

Contract parsing and validating the declared Microcks identity could initially happen in the Aspire layer, avoiding an OpenAPI dependency in the core topology package.

## Why `ServiceRef`, not `ApiRef`

These services are outside the integration system. The topology does not own or deploy their provider.

That makes them different from an internal API:

```text
ApiRef       component → component
ServiceRef   component → external platform service
```

The existing historical `MockApi` concept skipped an internal provider component. Here there is no provider component to skip.

## Deliberate exclusions

The first DSL should not include:

- `.WithMicrocks(...)`;
- mock response selection;
- latency or fault configuration;
- adapter configuration;
- stateful idempotency behavior;
- production image or Helm details;
- real-service fallback.

Those can be added later without changing the central `.Uses(ServiceRef)` model.

One adjustment I would make to the existing `feat/platform-services` direction: avoid defining a service as `Service.Container(image, port)`. A platform-service dependency should be an identity; containers and mocks are environment-specific ways of resolving it.

Understood. I’ll treat `feat/platform-services` as dead and non-authoritative.

The DSL design will be greenfield around:

- `ServiceRef` as a Dapr app-ID identity;
- per-component `.Uses(ServiceRef)`;
- separate `IDevelopmentDefinition`;
- local `Mock(...).FromOpenApi(...)` resolution;
- generated development-only `HTTPEndpoint` resources.

No compatibility with that branch’s types, model, or decisions is required.

